# notify-routes.default.tab — the SPEC §16 event→route table (issue #103).
#
# THE ONE source of truth for the shipped default routing. bootstrap/notify.sh reads it
# directly on the control node; ansible/roles/notify installs this same file on the PVE
# host at /usr/local/lib/provisioner/notify-routes.default.tab. There is no second copy
# of these defaults anywhere — change them here.
#
# ── FORMAT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   <event-glob>   <pushover: yes|no>   <priority: low|normal|high|emergency>   <once|->
#
# Whitespace-separated, `#` starts a comment, blank lines ignored.
#
# ── FIRST MATCH WINS — order IS the specificity rule ──────────────────────────
# The table is an ORDERED list, not the mapping SPEC §16 first sketched: a mapping gives
# no defensible precedence when two globs both match (`host.upgrade.failed` vs
# `host.upgrade.*`). So put the specific line ABOVE its family, and keep `*` last.
#
# ── `once` — the false-success guard ──────────────────────────────────────────
# `once` means "at most one send per notify SCOPE". run-pipeline.sh sets the scope to a
# per-cold-start token that SURVIVES the PVE 8→9 upgrade's reboots, so `pipeline.done`
# can physically fire only once across the three passes of a self-resuming cold start.
# It is defence in depth: the primary guarantee is that `pipeline.done` is emitted only
# at the true terminal milestone (see run-pipeline.sh main()).
#
# ── PER-ESTATE OVERRIDE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Either stage a replacement `notify-routes.tab` on the helper under
# the estate chroot's secrets/ (control-boot.sh pulls it to /etc/provisioner/notify-routes.tab),
# or set the `notify_routes` list in the estate's group_vars/vault and let
# ansible/roles/notify render it. Either wins over this file; neither is required.

# ── Terminal outcomes: the two events worth waking up for ─────────────────────
pipeline.failed        yes  high      -      # the estate died — #59's loud failure beacon
pipeline.done          yes  normal    once   # THE success ping. Exactly one per cold start.

# ── Host upgrade (SPEC §17) — emitted by the on-host state machine, at boot, with
# no control node alive. `failed` is high: a half-upgraded hypervisor at 3am.
host.upgrade.failed    yes  high      -
host.upgrade.done      yes  normal    -
host.upgrade.started   yes  low       -
host.upgrade.*         no   normal    -      # prechecks/reboot-imminent/resumed: log-only

# ── The needle (issue #103 scope extension) — the FIRST thing that can speak ──
# Emitted from a bare PVE host with no repo and no control node. `detached` is the moment
# the operator can walk away; `failed` is the only signal that exists at all if the needle
# dies, because nothing downstream is alive yet to report it.
host.needle.failed     yes  high      -
host.needle.detached   yes  normal    -      # NOT `once`: the needle has no persisted scope,
                                              # and it detaches exactly once per invocation.
host.needle.seeded     yes  low       -      # PROGRESS (issue #195): both seed VMs are restored and
                                              # the control node is being started. The one checkpoint
                                              # inside the needle's phase worth a ping — the image
                                              # pull is a multi-GB SFTP transfer and, until this
                                              # fired, `detached` → `controller.started` was the
                                              # longest silent window of the whole lap.
host.needle.*          no   normal    -

# ── Lifecycle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
controller.started     yes  low       once   # "the box is in control and driving" — ONCE, or a
                                              # three-pass 8→9 cold start would ping three times
                                              # and start training you to ignore the channel.
pipeline.handoff       no   normal    -      # a pass handed off to the upgrade — NOT a success
# ROUTED SINCE ISSUE #422, and the row above is why the two are not the same event. A
# HAND-OFF is expected and self-healing (the §17 resume unit re-enters the pipeline after the
# reboot, so nobody has to do anything). An INTERRUPTION is nobody's plan and NOTHING recovers
# from it: the pass was killed — a host reboot, a Jenkins abort, an operator Ctrl-C — and the
# build simply stopped. On 2026-09-02 that happened 43 minutes into a cold start, this event
# was emitted log-only, and the estate sat half-built and silent for an hour while looking
# healthy from outside. The message names the STAGE it died in (run-pipeline.sh's EXIT trap);
# the estate rides the title, as on every row here.
#
# NORMAL, not high, and this is a judgement call worth stating. The commonest cause is a
# DELIBERATE abort — someone stopped a Jenkins job — and a high-priority 3am push for a thing
# the operator just did themselves is exactly how a channel gets muted (the same reasoning
# validation.failed carries). `pipeline.failed` keeps the high ground. Raising this is a
# one-word edit on this row if the deliberate-abort case ever stops being the common one.
pipeline.interrupted   yes  normal    -      # the pass was KILLED — see pipeline.resumed below
# THE OTHER HALF OF THAT PAIR (issue #435). Since the operator's 2026-09-03 decision on
# issue #422 — report, notify AND resume — an interrupted lap ARMS a one-shot that re-enters
# the pipeline when the control node comes back, in the same mode and action it died in.
# This event is that pass announcing itself, before it does any work.
#
# LOW, and the pairing is the reason. `pipeline.interrupted` is the bad news and keeps
# `normal`; this is the good news that follows it — the estate is fixing itself and there is
# nothing to do. On Pushover `low` arrives without a sound: it is there in the list when the
# operator looks, next to the interruption it answers, and the pass's own `pipeline.done` or
# `pipeline.failed` (still `normal`/`high`) says how it ended. Routed rather than log-only
# because an estate that silently re-enters a two-hour build is precisely the "different
# estate to operate" the decision was held open over: if it restarts itself, it says so.
#
# NOT `once`, deliberately, and for the same reason as the progress rows: `once` latches on
# the cold-start notify scope, which SURVIVES a failed lap — so a second interruption in the
# same cold start would resume in complete silence, and that is the run being watched hardest.
pipeline.resumed       yes  low       -      # the control node came back and is re-running the lap
pipeline.started       no   normal    -
pipeline.exit          no   normal    -      # a pass ended rc=0 without reaching the terminal

# ── PROGRESS: the healthy-lap feed (issues #192 + #195) ───────────────────────
# THE COMPLAINT THIS ANSWERS, in the operator's words: "only 3 notifications over an hour,
# I have no idea what's happening". A cold start took ~an hour and pushed exactly three
# times — needle detached, controller driving, estate up — so between minute 2 and minute
# 58 a lap that was going PERFECTLY was indistinguishable from a lap that had wedged.
#
# These rows are the fix, and they are ALL about the SUCCESS path. The failure path was
# never the gap: `pipeline.failed` (high) has covered it since #59. What was missing is the
# ordinary "still going, here is where we are" ping, so every row below fires when a stage
# has just SUCCEEDED — and each one's detail also names the stage starting next, which is
# what turns a list of pings into a progress bar (see run-pipeline.sh main()).
#
# ONE PING PER STAGE TRANSITION — no finer. A full lap now costs the operator ~10 pushes
# instead of 3, which is the whole budget: the next thing after "I can't see anything" is
# "stop buzzing my phone", and §16 exists to keep this channel worth reading. In
# particular the three golden templates get ONE summary ping between them, not one each
# (they finish minutes apart and the per-template detail is already in the steps feed).
#
# LOW, deliberately. These are informational, and on Pushover `low` means it appears
# without a sound — you look when you look. The two rows that are allowed to interrupt
# you stay where they were: `pipeline.failed` (high) and `pipeline.done` (normal).
# Raising one of these is a one-word edit on its row.
#
# NOT `once`, ALSO deliberately, and this is the row-for-row difference from
# `controller.started` above. `once` is keyed on the cold-start notify scope, and that
# scope deliberately SURVIVES a failed lap so the retry still yields one success ping
# (run-pipeline.sh notify_scope()) — so a `once` progress row would make the RE-RUN of a
# failed cold start completely silent, which is precisely the run the operator is watching
# hardest. The cost of leaving it off is small and honest: on a three-pass 8→9 upgrade
# cold start, `host.prep.done` pings on each pass, and that repetition is true — host-prep
# really did run three times.
#
# THEY FIRE IN STEADY MODE TOO. Emission is unconditional (§16: call sites emit, the table
# decides), so a Jenkins-driven steady apply gets the same progress feed. That is a
# deliberate accept — an unattended 3am rebuild is exactly when it is wanted — and an
# estate that disagrees overrides this table per-estate rather than patching code.
host.prep.done         yes  low       -      # the PVE host is prepped; baking templates next
host.bake.done         yes  low       -      # the golden templates are ready; router next (ONE
                                              # summary row for server + opnsense-baked + desktop)
# The rest of the progress family lives with its own subsystem, still ordered above that
# subsystem's `*` line: opnsense.health.passed, vm.fleet.created, vm.fleet.provision.done
# and validation.started, below.

# The fleet upgrade's per-machine report (#1185): one push per update-fleet walk, machines
# numbered and never named (the fleet's composition does not leave the node).
fleet.upgrade.report   yes  normal    -

# The restart job's one push (#1184): machines numbered, never named.
fleet.reboot.report    yes  normal    -

# Planned reboots (#1488): one warning ~10 minutes ahead of the host's window or the fleet's
# scheduled restart ("The host reboots at 18:30 (kernel update): every machine restarts"), and
# one "reboot due" notice when an update asked for a reboot that waits for a later window. Both
# are sent only when the reboot will really happen / is really waiting; neither carries an address.
reboot.warning         yes  normal    -
reboot.due             yes  normal    -

# ── Post-build validation (issue #123) — the lap's own user-POV checks ────────
# `validation.failed` means the estate BUILT but does not behave the way a user expects.
# NORMAL, not high: in the advisory posture the lap still succeeds, `pipeline.done` carries
# the counts, and the checks are new enough that a 3am high-priority push over a fussy check
# would train the operator to ignore the channel — the exact failure mode §16 is written to
# avoid. Raise it here (one word) once the suite has earned it.
#
# "`pipeline.done` CARRIES THE COUNTS" WAS WRITTEN HERE LONG BEFORE IT WAS TRUE (issue #514).
# The completion ping said "Estate is up. Lap took …" and nothing about the checks, so this
# row was the only thing telling an operator that a check had gone red — two pushes for one
# lap outcome, the second landing seconds before the message meant to summarise it. Since
# #514 the completion really does carry "N passed, N failed, N skipped", and the stage emits
# this event RECORD-ONLY on any lap that will reach a completion.
#
# THE ROW STAYS ROUTED, AND IT IS NOT DEAD. Two kinds of lap never reach a completion to
# ride: the ENFORCING posture, where a red check ends the lap, and a §17 hand-off, where the
# whole chain runs and the pass deliberately declines to call itself finished. On those the
# announcement layer pushes this event itself, at the moment the ending is known, so a failed
# verdict can never end up with no signal at all. Flipping this row to `no` would re-open
# exactly that hole.
validation.failed      yes  normal    -
# `incomplete` is NOT a verdict and NOT a failure — the checks ABORTED on an infra fault before
# producing one, and under the advisory posture that is fully contained: the lap succeeded and
# nothing about the estate is known to be bad. LOG-ONLY on purpose (issue #288). `pipeline.done`
# lands seconds later carrying the sentence that puts it in context — "the build succeeded, only
# the post-build checks stopped early" — and it is the only ping that CAN say that, because it
# is the only one emitted after the lap is over. Routed here as well, this row would buy the
# operator two pushes for one outcome, which is exactly what happened on epimetheus 2026-08-21:
# the same "no verdict" sentence twice, a minute apart. Kept as its OWN row rather than left to
# fall through `validation.*` so that flipping the family row can never silently re-create the
# duplicate; the call site also passes --record-only, so this row states the intent as much as
# it enforces it. MUST stay above `validation.*` — first match wins.
validation.incomplete  no   normal    -
# `cleanup_failed` is NOT a verdict: the validation could not remove its own throwaway witness
# VM, so a machine is left on the estate holding a services-tier address (issue #128). Routed
# rather than log-only because it needs a HAND — one `qm destroy` — and the next lap's reap is a
# safety net, not a fix. NORMAL for the same reason validation.failed is: it is not a 3am wake-up.
validation.cleanup_failed yes normal  -
# `started` is the PROGRESS row for the lap's last stage (issue #195): everything is built,
# the estate is now being asked to prove it works. `done` stays LOG-ONLY on purpose — the
# verdict rides pipeline.done, which lands seconds later with the same counts, and two pings
# a minute apart saying the same thing is exactly the fragmentation #195's budget forbids.
# (That claim was also ahead of the code until issue #514 — see the `validation.failed`
# block above. The completion ping now carries the three counts on every lap that ran the
# suite, which is what this row and that one have both promised all along.)
validation.started     yes  low       -
validation.*           no   normal    -      # done: log-only, the verdict rides pipeline.done

# ── A commit just earned a "passed" mark on this estate (issue #1025, under #916/#915) ──
# `autotest.passed` is composed the one time `record_autotest_pass` (stages/validate.py)
# actually writes the mark, and SENT at the run's clean ending (#1489: not when the checks
# merely pass; a steady run gets a no-link "needs a cold start" sentence). Never derived from
# a count or a log line read back later, for the same borrowed-premise reason the mark itself refuses four other ways. It is NEWS, not
# an approval: the wording says a version CAN be cut, and cutting one is still the Cut
# version Jenkins job (#1023) the operator runs by hand. NORMAL, not high — a passed test
# is not a 3am wake-up — and not `once`: each lap that writes a mark names its OWN commit,
# so there is nothing here for a scope latch to be guarding against.
autotest.passed        yes  normal    -

# ── The router came up (or is about to come up) with no configuration (issue #125)
# `opnsense.unconfigured` is the one the 2026-08-08 iapetus lap needed and did not have: the
# router VM is being created with NO first-boot config ISO, so it will boot the bare baked
# template — no VLANs, no addresses, no routing — and the whole lap is already lost. HIGH,
# because it is emitted ~90 minutes before the failure it guarantees, and acting on it saves
# every one of them. `health.failed` is the same estate seen a few minutes later from the
# probe's side: NORMAL, not high — the gate's original design (SPEC §4.3) called for
# bootstrap to proceed best-effort there, but a failed gate now ENDS the lap in both bootstrap
# and steady state (operator decision 2026-08-08, issue #125; the bootstrap arm was the one
# still best-effort until then), so `health.failed` fires seconds before the die and
# `pipeline.failed` still owns the terminal beacon.
opnsense.unconfigured  yes  high      -
opnsense.health.failed yes  normal    -
# The PROGRESS row for the router stage (issue #195), and the reason it is the health GATE
# and not the tofu apply: "the router VM exists" is not a milestone anybody can act on —
# #125 is the whole story of a router that existed and routed nothing. All three probe
# layers green is the first moment the estate has a working gateway.
opnsense.health.passed yes  low       -
opnsense.*             no   normal    -      # seed.stale-state &c: log-only, it rides the steps feed

# ── The cross-job state lock (issue #79) ─────────────────────────────────────
# `state.lock.stale` means a previous job DIED holding the estate's apply lock and this run
# took it over after the TTL. Routed rather than log-only because it is the one lock event
# that says something happened that nobody saw: a job disappeared mid-apply, and whatever it
# was doing to the fleet stopped half-done. NORMAL, not high — the takeover already un-wedged
# the estate, so there is nothing to do at 3am; it is a "look at the previous lap" signal.
# A BLOCKED run needs no route of its own: it dies, and pipeline.failed already carries it.
state.lock.stale       yes  normal    -
state.lock.*           no   normal    -

# ── The fleet's two PROGRESS rows (issue #195) ───────────────────────────────
# SPEC §16 asked for the VM paths to be over-instrumented now and routed log-only so that
# "adding a coffee later is a one-line config edit, not a code change". This is that edit,
# for exactly two of them — and they are the two that bracket the longest stage in the lap:
# `vm.fleet.created` says the VMs exist and in-VM configuration (42 minutes on the
# 2026-08-14 lap, the single window with the least visibility) is starting; the matching
# `provision.done` says it finished. Everything else in the `vm.*` families stays log-only,
# because a ping per VM is how you get taught to ignore the channel.
# NOTE these two must stay ABOVE the family rows below them — first match wins.
vm.fleet.created       yes  low       -
vm.fleet.provision.done yes low       -

# ── Over-instrumented families, routed log-only today (SPEC §16: "flip later is a
# one-line CONFIG edit, not a code change").
vm.*.created           no   normal    -
vm.*.provision.*       no   normal    -
vm.*                   no   normal    -

# ── Pinned-version staleness (issue #6) — emitted by bootstrap/check-pins.sh ──
# A WEEKLY REPORT, NEVER AN ACTION. The checker only reads: it compares the versions this
# repo pins (docker/images.yaml, the rclone/player role defaults, genesis.sh's OPNsense
# series) against each project's own upstream release feed and says what is behind. Nothing
# is ever upgraded — everything we pin is pinned precisely so the operator picks the moment.
#
# `outdated` is NORMAL and NOT `once`. Normal because a stale pin is a this-week decision,
# not a 3am wake-up (`pipeline.failed` keeps that ground); and emphatically NOT `once`,
# because `once` latches per notify SCOPE and would turn the whole point of a recurring
# report into a single lifetime ping — the second week's new release would be silent.
# The checker already batches every stale pin into ONE message, so a busy week costs one
# push, not one per project.
maintenance.pin.outdated     yes  normal    -
# `unreachable` means the checker established NOTHING this run — every upstream lookup
# failed. Routed, at low, for the reason the `once` rows exist: a blind reporter that stays
# silent is indistinguishable from a clean bill of health, and this channel's whole value is
# that silence means "checked, nothing to do". Low because there is nothing to fix on the
# estate — it is "the report you are expecting is not coming".
maintenance.pin.unreachable  yes  low       -

# ── Expiring credentials (issue #852, under #847) — emitted by the weekly
# bootstrap/check-credentials.sh via bootstrap/credential_warn.py, one event PER
# CREDENTIAL so that each has its own route, its own journal line and its own history.
#
# `dead` and `unusable` are HIGH, and they are the only maintenance rows that are: a
# credential the issuing service has already REFUSED, or one that is staged and cannot be
# used as written, is not a this-week decision like a stale pin — it is a thing that is
# broken now and whose blast radius grows (a dead DNS token takes the router's certificate
# with it about two months later, silently). `expiring` is NORMAL — there is by definition
# still headroom, which is the whole point of warning ahead of the failure.
#
# ── NEITHER IS `once`, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART OF THIS BLOCK ────────
# notify.sh's `once` latch is set on ATTEMPT, not on delivery (its step 2 comment says so,
# and for `pipeline.done` that is right). Here it would be a lie: when the dead credential
# IS the notification credential, the first week's send fails, the latch is set anyway, and
# every following week reports `suppressed-duplicate` — silent, green, recorded as handled.
# So credential_warn.py keeps its OWN latch, written only when notify.sh's journal confirms
# the push was accepted, and re-sends every week until it actually travels. Adding `once`
# to these rows re-opens exactly that hole; the warner detects it and turns the build red
# rather than letting it be silent, but do not put it back.
#
# The anti-nag guarantee is still there and is stronger: one push per credential per
# CALENDAR MONTH, and each message says so in its own last sentence.
maintenance.credential.dead.*      yes  high      -
maintenance.credential.expiring.*  yes  normal    -
# `unusable` (#856): the credential is STAGED where it belongs and this node cannot use
# what is there — an empty or half-written file, a key with no value, a directory where the
# file should be. HIGH, with `dead`, and for the same reason: nothing is going to fix
# itself, and if it is the notification credential then this row is what would have carried
# the news. It is its own event rather than a second meaning for `dead` because no service
# refused anything, and `dead`'s message says one did.
maintenance.credential.unusable.*  yes  high      -
# The blind run — the check reached NONE of the issuing services, so it knows nothing
# about any credential and deliberately warns about none of them. Routed low for the same
# reason maintenance.pin.unreachable is: a reporter that goes quiet when it is blind reads
# exactly like a clean bill of health.
maintenance.credential.unreachable yes  low       -
# Anything else under this family is log-only — a new sub-event cannot start pushing
# merely by being invented. MUST stay below the three rows above; first match wins.
maintenance.credential.*           no   normal    -

# ── Jenkins host-level self-update (issue #265) — emitted by the control node's
# provisioner-jenkins-update.service, a systemd timer OUTSIDE the Jenkins container.
# It rebuilds the derived image from the repo's pin at a per-estate quiet hour, recreates
# the container ONLY if that image actually changed, health-checks it and rolls back on
# failure. So these rows describe an unattended change to the CONTROL PLANE ITSELF, and
# they are ordered by "how much of it is still working", not by how dramatic they sound.
#
# `failed` is the only one worth waking up for, and it is the narrowest: Jenkins answered
# neither on the new image NOR after the rollback, i.e. the UI the operator would use to
# investigate anything else is gone. HIGH.
maintenance.jenkins.failed       yes  high      -
# `rolled_back` means the net WORKED — the new image was unhealthy, the previous one is
# back, Jenkins is serving. Nothing is broken at 3am; what is owed is a look at the pin
# that produced the bad image before the next window. NORMAL, the same reasoning as
# validation.failed: a high-priority push for a self-corrected fault is how this channel
# gets muted.
maintenance.jenkins.rolled_back  yes  normal    -
# `build_failed` is the mildest bad outcome in the whole mechanism: the rebuild died, so
# NOTHING was touched and the container is still serving the image it was serving a second
# earlier. Routed anyway (not log-only) for the reason maintenance.pin.unreachable is: a
# silent updater is indistinguishable from an updater that had nothing to do, and this
# channel's value is that silence means "checked, nothing needed". NORMAL, not low,
# because unlike the pin reporter this one is failing on THIS estate's own build.
maintenance.jenkins.build_failed yes  normal    -
# `updated` — the control plane was replaced under you last night and came back healthy.
# LOW: informational, no sound, look when you look. It is genuinely rare (the usual
# outcome is `current`, which sends nothing), so it is not push budget the operator will
# resent — and being told "your Jenkins is not the one you left" is worth one silent line.
maintenance.jenkins.updated      yes  low       -
# `current` (nothing to do — no rebuild, no restart) and `deferred` (a pipeline was in
# flight, so the window was skipped) fall through to the family row below and stay
# LOG-ONLY. `current` is the nightly no-op and would be pure noise; `deferred` costs one
# day and the next window retries it, so a push would be asking for attention nobody
# needs to give.
maintenance.*                no   normal    -      # pin.current &c: log-only — a weekly
                                                    # "nothing to do" push is how a channel
                                                    # gets muted.

# ── The prometheus MCP server (issue #1014) — one call, one line ──────────────
# `mcp.call` fires once per tool call the AI makes through the server (#1055: "every call
# the MCP server makes... sends one plain Pushover line: which client, which job, which
# build"). NORMAL, not low: unlike the lap's own progress rows above, this is the one
# channel that says an AI touched the estate at all, so it stays audible rather than
# silent-on-Pushover. NOT `once` — every call is its own event.
cutversion.draft_ready   yes  normal    -      # a draft release is waiting for you to publish it (#1107)
mcp.call                 yes  normal    -
# `mcp.approval.pending` (#1053): a destructive job (restore/rebuild/coldstart) STARTED BY
# THE MCP SERVER is paused on Jenkins's own `input` gate and needs the operator's Proceed/
# Abort. HIGH — the one MCP event that is actually asking for a decision, and it is
# time-bounded (Jenkinsfile.{restore,rebuild,coldstart} time out that input after an hour).
mcp.approval.pending     yes  high      -
# `approval.granted` (#1463): every approval of a destructive job's input step (cold start,
# rebuild, restore, nested rebuild) pings once the approver has answered, naming job, build,
# approver account and estate. HIGH: self-approval by the assistant is accepted (SPEC 6.7) and
# this ping is its safeguard. Not `once`: every approval is its own event.
approval.granted         yes  high      -

# ── Signs-of-compromise check (issue #1057) — emitted by bootstrap/security_check.py ──
# The job only sends this when it FOUND something present live and not declared, and it is
# silent on a clean run, so every arrival is actionable. HIGH: a possible intrusion is the
# thing a phone is for. Without this row the event fell to the `*` catch-all and the first
# live run logged `route=log-only` -- an anomaly that paged nobody. NOT `once`: each daily
# run that still finds drift should say so.
security.anomaly.found yes  high      -

# ── Catch-all: anything not named above is log-only. Keep this line LAST. ──────
*                      no   normal    -
